### Page 3 of 7

```text
third and final reading, seconded by Mr. Pierson.  Upon inquiry, Brandon Cammarata, Urban
Planning Office, reviewed state statutory annexation criteria and staff findings, and confirmed
there is nothing in the annexation action itself that triggers infrastructure costs to nearby property
owners. Discussion followed and included: Concerns with train crossing traffic (safety and
volume), emergency responder access, potential issues involving a final plat, and state statute
criteria.  Ms. Case raised a procedural question concerning passing the ordinance on 3 and final
rd
reading when governing body members have changed (i.e. newly elected members recently
taking office) and whether there was any conflict or issue with new members acting in their
official capacity with regard to carry-over of continuing City Council business items.  Upon
inquiry, Dan White, City Attorney, advised he had reviewed Robert’s Rules of Order pertaining
to whether pending business left over upon adjournment of a previous City Council is resumed at
the next meeting of City Council, and stated the language does not apply to City Council as it is
not a legislative assembly or a board, but that it is somewhat unique as it has both legislative and
executive functions.  He cited a past Kentucky Supreme Court ruling that ruled on the
importance of a City Council to act as a continuous body to be able to work on unfinished
business and to conduct its business expeditiously, and further noted information found in
McQuillin’s Treatise on Municipal Corporations which states a governing body is considered to
be continuous and it is in the public’s interest that City Council’s be able to conduct business
expeditiously.  Mr. White opined that the City Council is permitted to proceed with all of the
items on the meeting agenda.  Comments followed on the City’s adoption of Robert’s Rules of
Order, and City fire and rescue response to the proposed annexation area. Motion carried.
Voting “yes” - all members of the governing body with the exception of Ms. Ash, Ms. Case, and
Mr. Valdez voting “no.”  (#3840)
