### Page 4 of 7

```text
PORTION OF SECTION 1, 12, AND 17, T.13N., R.67W., 6
 P.M., LARAMIE COUNTY,
TH
WY (LOCATED SOUTH OF I-80 AND PREDOMINANTLY EAST OF SOUTHWEST
DRIVE).”  Expressing concerns were:  Victoria Sopr, who referenced a handout she provided at
the Public Services Committee meeting, John Fafoutakis, Paula Qualls, Leo Garcia, Donn
Edmunds, Jon Ware and Rawley Marrs. Topics presented included:  Concerns with potential
future infrastructure costs to nearby property owners; property values; train crossing traffic and
congestion issues (Clerk’s note: Letters were read citing train/traffic statistics); emergency
responder concerns; increased traffic due to construction when property is developed and
affiliated construction conditions; health and safety issues; access points, and financial burden
involving infrastructure reimbursements for those on fixed incomes. Mike Basom, Dray,
Thomson & Dyekman law firm, representing the developer, expressed support for a 30 day
postponement of the item, as recommended by the Public Services Committee, to provide time
to continue negotiations with the railroad.  Mr. Brown moved to adopt, seconded by Mr. Collins.
Mr. Brown moved to postpone for 30 days (to Feb. 9, 2009), seconded by Mr. Pierson.  Gay
Woodhouse, attorney representing residents in the area, spoke in opposition to annexation
generally and the motion to postpone, citing her concerns with state statute criteria and
Burlington Northern railroad property inclusion in compilation of the contiguity percentage.
John Fafoutakis spoke in opposition to postponement unless the developer would build a suitable
alternate access to the railroad grade crossing area.  Following comments pertaining to
postponement(s), motion to postpone carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of the governing
body with the exception of Ms. Ash, Ms. Case, and Mr. Valdez voting “no”.
“A RESOLUTION DESIGNATING THE SUNDAYS DURING 2009 THAT ANNUAL
LIQUOR LICENSEES MAY EXTEND DISPENSING ROOM HOURS OF OPERATION FOR
SALES UNTIL 2:00 A.M. THE FOLLOWING DAY.”  Upon inquiry by John Fafoutakis,
Mayor Kaysen advised the proposed resolution would approve extension of liquor licensees’
Sunday hours from 10:00 a.m. to 2:00 a.m. the following day on 4 Sundays during 2009 (i.e.
Feb. 1, May 24, July 19 and 26).  Upon inquiry, Chief Bob Fecht, Police Department, advised
recently released driving while intoxicated arrest statistics reflects a significant increase in
volume, and provided statements on the annual 4 day request generally.  J.J. Moran, President,
Laramie County Liquor Association, spoke in favor of the extended Sunday hours as an option
for licensees to remain open longer on the approved days, if desired, as provided by state statute.
Brian Grzegorczyk, owner of Alf’s Pub and Package Liquor, also spoke in support of the
resolution, and provided information on charitable programs numerous bar owners are involved
in and how fund-raising efforts give back to the community.  Mr. Valdez reported there was no
recommendation on the item from Finance Committee.  Mr. Collins moved to adopt, seconded
by Mr. Brown. Upon inquiry, Mr. Moran provided a brief  review on the history of the Sunday
hours extension request, and Carol Intlekofer, City Clerk, advised the statute is not specific to
Sundays and can be applicable to other days of the week.  Comments included liquor sales
revenue; option by licensees to remain open longer on the approved Sundays; hours of operation,
and statutory wording indicating the governing body “may” approve. Motion carried.  Voting
“yes” – all members of the governing body with the exception of Ms. Broyles, Mr. Pierson and
Mr. Spiker voting “no”. (#5095)
